      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T23:39:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s a potential issue introduced with this change.\n\nThe initialization code sets all power signal GPIOS to the inactive state at startup.  When PCH_RSMRST is configured as active high, then initial state of the pin will be physical 0, which asserts reset to the AP.\n\nBut if PCH_RSMRST is configured as active low, the initial state of the pin will be physical 1.  So this will cause a bounce on PCH_RSMRST between the init code and the first time the passthru handler runs.\n\nWe have a similar problem with the SYS_RST signal, which is currently configured as active low.  We may need a DT property to mark the power signals that are reset style signals.  These signals should be logically asserted at init time.",
      "revId": "17079caf116a1c7c42a703b821814f7439f0e9ba",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "423864cc_20e1561b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-12T17:44:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure if you saw this comment.  Once PCH_RSMRST is configured as active low, the init code will release PCH_SMRST on a cold boot:\nhttps://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/HEAD:src/platform/ec/zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_gpio.c;l\u003d150\n\nThe reset signal won\u0027t be asserted until the first time `rsmrst_pass_thru_handler()` is called.",
      "parentUuid": "54e408c1_c1d18fb1",
      "revId": "17079caf116a1c7c42a703b821814f7439f0e9ba",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
